`bin/ardb` has an `=head1 ENVIRONMENT` section that documents exactly one variable:

    WWW_ARDB_DEBUG

But the code reads two more:

- `WWW_ARDB_NO_CACHE` — read in `WWW::ARDB::CLI::no_cache` as the default for the `--no-cache` flag.
- `WWW_ARDB_JSON` — read in `WWW::ARDB::CLI::json` as the default for the `--json` flag.

Someone who sets `WWW_ARDB_JSON=1` gets JSON output with no way to learn it from the documentation.

What to do:
1. Extend the `=head1 ENVIRONMENT` section in `bin/ardb` with `WWW_ARDB_NO_CACHE` and `WWW_ARDB_JSON`: one line each, what it does and what its default is. Use the `=env NAME` shortcut form, one variable per block, as the POD conventions require.
2. Check whether the same three variables are documented anywhere else (the CLI class POD, the WWW::ARDB class POD) and make the statement consistent — the CLI class already documents its `debug`, `no_cache` and `json` attributes, the binary does not.
3. Confirm with `ardb --help` that the help text still lists the three global flags; the help text is hardcoded in `WWW::ARDB::CLI::execute` and must not drift from the real flags.

Documentation only — no behaviour change, no `Changes` entry.

